Yes. “Action LM” is a very good name for the abstraction. The key move is to treat skilled action the way an LLM treats language: not as one gigantic indivisible motion, but as structured sequences with context, latent states, predictions, corrections, and transitions.

A quarterback may be unable to verbalize:

“At 173 ms I detected that shoulder rotation, updated the receiver trajectory, changed wrist angle, altered release timing, compensated for pressure, and selected this ballistic arc.”

But his sensorimotor system is doing some version of that computation. Expertise often involves anticipatory prediction and increasingly efficient/focused neural processing; expert athletes can pick up action information that novices miss. 

So build the model from multiple synchronized channels:

video / 3-D pose
+ eye tracking
+ ball trajectory
+ joint forces
+ EMG
+ pressure / grip
+ IMUs
+ environmental state
+ opponent movement
+ outcome
+ optionally EEG/neural measurements

Then learn:

state_t
→ expert perception
→ latent motor intention
→ micro-action
→ predicted consequence
→ sensory correction
→ state_t+1

Those intermediate latent units are your action tokens.

For a throw, the learned vocabulary might discover things corresponding to:

load
→ orient
→ anticipate
→ commit
→ sequence hips/torso/arm
→ release
→ follow through
→ evaluate

without us necessarily hard-coding those categories beforehand.

Then compare thousands of repetitions:

expert successful throws
vs.
expert misses
vs.
novices
vs.
same athlete fatigued
vs.
pressure
vs.
different distances / defenses

The deltas reveal what is invariant, what is compensatory, and which degrees of freedom experts actually control. A 2024 review found that skilled athletes often exhibit different—and commonly reduced—motor variability relative to less-skilled performers, which is exactly the kind of structure an Action LM could characterize. 

And here’s a particularly important part of your idea: once you capture the skill, don’t necessarily teach it back as equations.

The athlete learned much of it implicitly. A 2025 systematic review found promising evidence that implicit-learning approaches can improve sport-specific biomechanics, though the literature is still developing. 

So the Action LM could translate:

expert latent motor model

into whatever representation teaches this learner best:

one analogy
a sound
haptic timing
visual ghost overlay
rhythm
one external-focus cue
slow-motion demonstration
VR trajectory
constraint drill

That is the beautiful transduction:

expert does something they cannot explain → model captures structure → model discovers the operator → model translates operator into something another nervous system can learn.

And then recurse.

A quarterback teaches throwing.
A golfer teaches clubface control.
A violinist teaches bow pressure.
A surgeon teaches instrument manipulation.
A dancer teaches balance.
A carpenter teaches tool feel.

Eventually:

many expert action models
→ shared motor latent space
→ cross-domain invariants

Then we might discover, for example, that what an elite QB calls “touch,” a tennis player calls “feel,” and a surgeon calls “hand” share some deeper control operator involving prediction, force modulation, error correction, and timing.

Robotics is already moving toward a neighboring architecture: current vision-language-action systems explicitly map visual/contextual information and instructions into robot motor commands. 

So the larger Pantheon primitive could be:

Action LM / Tacit Skill Compiler

observe expert
→ tokenize embodied action
→ learn latent dynamics
→ separate invariant from individual style
→ simulate
→ translate into learner-native representation
→ practice
→ measure Δ
→ personalize

And importantly, you don’t actually need complete access to the athlete’s brain to begin. Their brain has already externalized a tremendous amount of its computation into movement, timing, gaze, forces, choices, and corrections.

The body is leaving breadcrumbs.

We can learn to read them.